WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.419 align:start position:0%
 
I'm Sienna Cregg  I'm an anthropologist who teaches and  works at Dartmouth College I'm a medical  anthropologist so I focus on  generally speaking cross-cultural  approaches to health and illness global  health women's and children's health and  I work in Tibetan Himalaya I've been  very fortunate in my career so far that  I've had the opportunity to live and  work in Lhasa and to be in the Tibet  Autonomous Region as well as in qinghai  province Tibetan area and to have this  long-standing relationship with Nepal  there are extremely different political  climates and social worlds really but  they also have a lot of similarities in  terms of the challenges that are facing  regions today living and working in  Lhasa involves a fair amount of  self-censorship and a navigation of  daily life that always feels constrained  and yet there's so much creativity and  wonderful work being done there as well  it's just that it's very understated I  felt very fortunate to be working in  some of the premier Tibetan medical  hospitals in China and to see the the  depth of knowledge of the practitioners  there as well as the real care and  attention put forward toward the future  of Tibetan medicine under great  constraint in Nepal I would say the same  is true but they're working with  different constraints they're the  doctors that I work with are struggling  to be recognized in a state context that  has historically marginalized them and  the people and communities from which  they come and they are trying to figure  out their own place in a shifting not  just political landscape but in economic  one in both cases there are questions  about future generations but I would say  in Nepal the questions about whether or  not or in what way a new generation of  practitioners will emerge is a more  difficult one than in that in the  Tibetan region in part because  in Tibet there are institutions that  people can now belong to and imagine a  career path in whereas in Nepal that's  much more difficult Tibetan medicine is  incredibly adaptive there is I would say  a core set of theories theoretical  principles and texts that relate to  those principles that stretch from the  refugee communities in South India all  the way to Mongolia Buryatia and into  the West and Tibetan doctors practicing  there so there is a something that is  shared or something that is communicable  across all of these domains and yet in  each space we're so Arriba or the the  science of healing as it's often  translated from the Tibetan is practiced  there's a huge amount of variety  variation in practices variation in  local lineage based traditions that  inform how practice is done how  medicines are made and then there is  perforce a huge amount of variation in  the materia medica that is used Tibetan  medicine has a very complex and  incredibly adaptive set of principles  for how you substitute things if you  can't find X well you look for something  that is y that has the same role or  taste and post digestive taste potency  and those things also can cross species  as well so you could have a plant that  that at the level of tastes and potency  is mimicked by an animal product or the  other way around in many ways it is  possible to be a trans Himalayan doctor  to travel between worlds really between  the Nepal or the Indian Himalaya and the  Tibetan Plateau but it is tricky  especially the translation or travel  between India and China people find  spaces to meet doctors from Dharamsala  and other areas of the Tibetan exile  community and those from China have in  recent years been able to come together  in conference formats or in other  settings and share notes  about their historical trajectories talk  about the common problems that they face  especially around the standardization of  practice of Education and the problems  that are arising from the scaling up of  Industry around Tibetan medicine and  what that means for the materia medica  that the that the tradition depends on  folks from Nepal though have in some  senses more ability to move back and  forth across the border or at least they  they have I've had many doctors from  Nepal come and visit institutions in  Lhasa and visit institutions in shining'  which is the capital of Ching hai  province but they are more constrained  usually financially and so that travel  is dependent on a network of sponsorship  and and relationships that aren't quite  as constraining as it is for colleagues  in China so on the one hand Tibetan  medicine is becoming a global commodity  it's very much a commodity in East Asia  and it's becoming much more so in Europe  and North America and Japan on the other  hand Tibetan medicine is still the  frontline of health care for many many  people across the Tibetan Plateau in the  Himalaya and there's a fair amount of  dissonance in those two realities in  many ways Tibetan medicine could be  viewed as something that stitches a very  large and very diverse community  together that said the Tibetan medicine  that you find in Ladakh is going to be  quite different than the Tibetan  medicine you find in Bhutan but yes  there is a certain commitment to ways of  viewing the body ways of understanding  what it means to be ill why you get ill  which is you know it's it's a it's a  very basic human set of principles and  concerns really but it does provide a  kind of vocabulary both an embodied  vocabulary and a spoken one and a  written one that that can in many ways  provide a connection across this this  very diverse area to be a Himalayan  healer in a place like Mustang Nepal  where I've worked now for many years  requires a huge array of skills and  abilities it's not just that you are  capable of getting up at 4:00 in the  morning because someone has come to call  and their mother is sick and you get on  your horse and you ride off to help that  person with your little bag of of  powders and pills it's also that you  come back from that early morning  adventure and you sit down and you try  with your nephew or your cousin to write  an email in English to the sponsor who  has paid for the new roof on your school  to communicate needs that go well beyond  medicine it also means that you have to  wear many hats in the course of village  life you have to participate in all of  the expected rituals and other daily  rules that that are required of you as  you know either a monk or la tantric  household or priest in the context of  Mustang but you also have to be  attentive to so many other needs and  questions you have to you have to also  be thinking about the future of the  people that you're training and how  different their world is and their  professional and adult life will be from  the world that you've grown up in and  are quite comfortable navigating one of  the things I would really like to  continue to pursue in my work over the  coming decade or so is to try to track  and understand and productively engage  with communities and people that I've  known now for many years from Nepal who  are now living in places as nearby as  New York and in other parts of the world  in other parts of South Asia and to  think with them about what it means to  have a sense of trans local home at this  point that what this means for an aging  population who now might not might have  financial security but might not have  their family with them anymore what this  means for a new generation of young  people who are growing up with an  allegiance to a place like Mustang but  once or twice removed how they  understand their place in this  that place and at the same time my  interest in in these processes connect  back to my core interests in health and  illness I'm very curious about what the  future of Tibetan medicine will be in  the places where I've been working for a  long time but also how people in places  like New York navigate and make sense of  health and illness issues in this very  new set of circumstances

